According to scientific and logical wisdom, yes; dead things do not tend =
to stop being dead. But that logic does not make it definitive. If you =
are using that statement in, for instance, a story in a fantastical =
setting where resurrection is commonplace, the implications of the lack =
of clarity become much clearer.
=20
"I haven't seen Bob for a few days."
"Oh, yeah, he has been dead."
=20
See how easily that could say "he has been asleep" or "he has been =
eating". Both of those examples imply that Bob is no longer asleep or =
eating. Just because logic dictates meaning, doesn't mean the syntax is =
as implicit.

